  <h2>The situation</h2>
  <p>Like most trades businesses, this one ran through the owner. Quotes, orders and questions all came to his phone, and the website did little more than list a number to call. He needed a brand that looked like the work he does, and a storefront that works like a good counter clerk: customers figure out what they need, get a price and order, with AI doing real work behind it.</p>

  <h2>Designing the identity with AI</h2>
  <p>The brand took four days and a lot of iteration. I used Claude as a design partner through every step I would normally run with a studio team:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Research.</strong> A competitive map of regional shops and a mood board built from real steel: mill scale, galvanizing, powder coat.</li>
    <li><strong>A surface study.</strong> How bare, galvanized and powder-coated steel actually read on screen, so the brand colors come from the material instead of a trend.</li>
    <li><strong>Letterform research.</strong> Monogram and stencil studies, then a wordmark drawn to be manufactured. The bridges in the letters are sized to what a torch and the sheet can hold.</li>
    <li><strong>Iteration at speed.</strong> Many rounds across Claude, Illustrator and Figma. AI widened the options and pressure-tested them; I made the calls.</li>
  </ul>
