  <p>AI didn't design the brand. It made it possible for one person to do a studio's worth of exploring in a week, and to spend the saved time on the decisions that matter.</p>

  <h2>Building the storefront</h2>
  <p>The storefront is live at <a href="https://guntermetals.com/">guntermetals.com</a>.</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>An opening that shows the craft.</strong> The storefront opens with the wordmark being cut from a steel sheet, then gets out of the way so customers can shop.</li>
    <li><strong>Ordering in shop language.</strong> A phone-first flow where a customer picks the metal, the shape and the size, then types a cut list the way they'd say it at the counter, with live totals.</li>
    <li><strong>One lead pipeline.</strong> Service and commercial estimate requests land in a single queue, so nothing lives only in a text message.</li>
    <li><strong>A catalog AI can read.</strong> Product and pricing rules sit in plain, tested files, so the same logic serves the website, AI shopping assistants and future tools for contractors.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Why it matters for your business</h2>
  <p>The AI part only works because the business rules were written down first. What the shop takes on, how a cut list gets checked, and how work gets priced all became files every tool reads from. That's the same foundation the <a href="/kit/">free AI Foundation Kit</a> helps you build.</p>
